{"node":{"id":"HP:0002021","label":"Pyloric stenosis","ic":4.974,"definition":"Pyloric stenosis, also known as infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, is an uncommon condition in infants characterized by abnormal thickening of the pylorus muscles in the stomach leading to gastric outlet obstruction. Clinically infants are well at birth. Then, at 3 to 6 weeks of age, the infants present with projectile vomiting, potentially leading to dehydration and weight loss."},"diseases":[{"id":"MONDO:0019609","label":"Zellweger spectrum disorders","in_core":true,"edge":{"id":"e_c8dcdb82a602","source":"MONDO:0019609","target":"HP:0002021","type":"has_phenotype","evidence":[{"db":"HPO","origin":"Orphanet","record":"ORPHA:912 · ORPHA:912","url":"https://www.orpha.net/en/disease/detail/912","level":"curated","date":"2026-09-02","confidence":0.6,"snippet":"Zellweger syndrome: Pyloric stenosis (frequency HP:0040282) [TAS]"},{"db":"Orphanet","origin":"Orphanet","record":"ORPHA:912","level":"curated","date":"2026-06-23","url":"https://www.orpha.net/en/disease/detail/912","confidence":0.6,"snippet":"Pyloric stenosis — Frequent (79-30%)"}],"contradicting":[],"confidence":0.6,"independent_sources":["Orphanet"],"level":"curated"},"confidence":0.6},{"id":"MONDO:0010035","label":"Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome","in_core":false,"edge":{"id":"e_8a89e70121a9","source":"MONDO:0010035","target":"HP:0002021","type":"has_phenotype","evidence":[{"db":"HPO","origin":"OMIM","record":"OMIM:270400 · PMID:10807690;PMID:34349606","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10807690;PMID:34349606/","level":"observed","date":"2022-08-23","confidence":1.0,"snippet":"Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome: Pyloric stenosis (frequency 11/11) [PCS]"},{"db":"HPO","origin":"Orphanet","record":"ORPHA:818 · ORPHA:818","url":"https://www.orpha.net/en/disease/detail/818","level":"curated","date":"2026-09-02","confidence":0.3,"snippet":"Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome: Pyloric stenosis (frequency HP:0040283) [TAS]"},{"db":"Orphanet","origin":